(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-valvular-heart-disease-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Valvular Heart Disease Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 22-year-old healthy woman has no symptoms. On a routine examination a mid-systolic click is heard at the apex with no murmur. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Mitral valve prolapse","Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse usually affects healthy women aged 15 to 30, is usually benign and asymptomatic, and gives a mid-systolic click at the apex as the leaflets flare into the left atrium. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man with known pulmonic stenosis now has exertional breathlessness and fatigue, and he fainted while running. Which process do these symptoms signal?","choices":["Left atrial enlargement and fibrillation","Acute leak from papillary muscle rupture","A benign finding needing no action","Right heart failure from severe stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mild to moderate pulmonic stenosis is usually asymptomatic; severe disease produces right heart failure symptoms such as exertional dyspnea, fatigue, angina and syncope. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"A 16-year-old boy has a harsh, high-pitched crescendo-decrescendo mid-systolic ejection murmur at the left second to fourth intercostal spaces at the sternal border. It grows louder with inspiration, and the second heart sound is split. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Pulmonic regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis","Aortic stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Harsh means stenosis and systolic narrows it to the aortic or pulmonic valve; the left upper sternal border, the inspiratory increase of a right-sided murmur and the split second sound mark pulmonic stenosis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 57, 8"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic mitral regurgitation has an echocardiogram showing a regurgitant fraction of 64%. Which severity does this indicate?","choices":["Mild regurgitation","Ungradeable by echocardiogram","Severe regurgitation","Moderate regurgitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mitral regurgitation is severe when the regurgitant fraction is 50% or more, meaning over half of the blood ejected goes backward. A fraction of 30 to 49% is moderate and below 30% is mild. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 50, 49"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration is examined further. Which additional finding is most expected?","choices":["Elevated jugular pulsations","A collapsing radial pulse","Delayed, weak carotid pulse","Head bobbing with each beat"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation raises the jugular venous pressure with visible neck pulsations, and there may be a venous thrill (a palpable vibration) over the right jugular vein. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man, three days after an acute myocardial infarction, has sudden breathlessness, crackles in both lungs and a new holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Papillary muscle rupture","Myxomatous degeneration","Rheumatic heart disease","Tricuspid annular dilation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An acute myocardial infarction with papillary muscle rupture is an acute cause of mitral regurgitation, and acute mitral regurgitation presents with left-sided heart failure symptoms such as dyspnea and rales. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"A 29-year-old man who injects drugs has fevers for two weeks. There is a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border near the epigastrium that grows louder with inspiration (Carvallo sign), and his neck veins pulsate. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tricuspid regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border near the epigastrium that increases with inspiration is tricuspid regurgitation; in an injection drug user the primary cause is infective endocarditis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 71, 70"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with mitral stenosis has worsening breathlessness. Echocardiogram shows a valve area of 0.8 cm2, a mean gradient of 14 mmHg and a peak velocity of 3.4 m/s. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","A loop diuretic indefinitely","Transcatheter aortic valve replacement","Afterload reduction with hydralazine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Her values are severe (area below 1.0 cm2, mean gradient above 10 mmHg, velocity above 3.0 m/s), and severe mitral stenosis with symptomatic dyspnea or pulmonary hypertension is an indication for percutaneous balloon valvotomy. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 42, 40"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with mitral valve prolapse has now developed symptomatic severe mitral regurgitation with a falling ejection fraction. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["A mitral clip or valve replacement","Reassurance with no treatment","Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","Aortic valve replacement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Prolapse usually needs no treatment, but if it progresses to mitral regurgitation the options are a clip, transcatheter mitral valve replacement, or biological or mechanical replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 54, 52"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man has a brief early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space from pulmonic regurgitation. Which maneuver makes the murmur louder?","choices":["Squatting","Standing","Expiration","Sitting upright"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases venous return increases the pulmonic regurgitation murmur -- inspiration, squatting and lying supine. Expiration and standing decrease it. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 63, 9"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a mechanical mitral valve asks whether she could take daily aspirin instead of her current anticoagulant. Which long-term therapy is recommended for her?","choices":["Aspirin in place of it","No drug after one year","Heparin every day","A vitamin K antagonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With a mechanical heart valve, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the one most commonly used. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man with hypertension has chronic aortic regurgitation, no symptoms and a preserved ejection fraction. Which is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","Emergency aortic valve replacement","Intravenous nitroprusside in intensive care","A loop diuretic and fluid restriction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic aortic regurgitation with preserved ejection fraction or no symptoms is managed medically with afterload reduction: an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker, a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker or hydralazine. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman has a holosystolic blowing murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. She stands up quickly from squatting while the examiner listens. How does the murmur change?","choices":["It does not change","It gains a click","It becomes softer","It becomes louder"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The mitral regurgitation murmur decreases with standing or the Valsalva maneuver and increases with squatting and handgrip, which raise resistance to ventricular ejection. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with known severe aortic stenosis has exertional chest pain. His physician is reviewing options before cardiology sees him. Which should be avoided for his chest pain?","choices":["Staying well hydrated","Cardiology consultation","Nitroglycerin","Resting when pain starts"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patients with aortic stenosis are preload dependent, and nitrates drop preload. If angina is present in aortic stenosis, nitrates are avoided; the same logic removes dehydration and strenuous exertion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with aortic stenosis on echocardiogram plays competitive tennis and often skips fluids on hot days. She asks what she should change. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Restrict fluids to keep her volume low","Increase training to strengthen her heart","No changes until symptoms appear","Avoid competitive sports and dehydration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic stenosis makes the patient preload dependent, so she should avoid strenuous physical activity and competitive sports and avoid dehydration and hypovolemia, both of which reduce the preload she relies on. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with a mechanical mitral valve takes warfarin and needs elective abdominal surgery that requires stopping it. Which is the most appropriate anticoagulation plan?","choices":["Double the warfarin","Bridge with heparin","Stop all anticoagulation","Switch to aspirin alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If surgery requires a transition to heparin, warfarin must be bridged: heparin covers the gap until the international normalized ratio is therapeutic again. This matters most with a mechanical valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has tricuspid regurgitation identified on an echocardiogram and now has pedal edema and ascites. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Balloon valvotomy now","Cardiology referral","Reassure; mild disease","Start lifelong warfarin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is referred when it is identified on echocardiogram or when there are right heart failure symptoms; pedal edema and ascites mark severe disease. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 72, 71"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has a mid-diastolic rumble at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration. Which electrocardiogram finding is most expected?","choices":["Right axis deviation","Left ventricular hypertrophy","Right atrial enlargement","Left atrial abnormality"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid stenosis obstructs inflow from the right atrium to the right ventricle, so the electrocardiogram shows right atrial enlargement out of proportion to right ventricular hypertrophy. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 68, 66"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a diastolic blowing murmur at the left upper sternal border. An echocardiogram is ordered to find out why the valve leaks. Which finding would explain her regurgitation?","choices":["Tricuspid annular dilation","Redundant mitral leaflets","A narrowed, immobile mitral valve","Aortic root dilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Echocardiography is used to determine the cause of aortic regurgitation, such as aortic root dilation or aortic dissection. Root disease is one of the two main groups of causes, with valve disease. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 29, 24"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman who had rheumatic fever as a child has breathlessness on exertion. At the apex there is a low-pitched rumbling diastolic murmur that begins with an opening snap (a sharp sound just after the second heart sound), best heard with the bell in the left lateral decubitus position (lying on the left side). Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Aortic regurgitation","Mitral regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Rumble means stenosis and diastolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the apex, the opening snap after the second heart sound and the left lateral position mark mitral stenosis. Rheumatic heart disease is its most common cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 38, 34"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has mitral stenosis found on an echocardiogram done before pregnancy. She has no symptoms and is in sinus rhythm. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Cardiology consultation","Start a loop diuretic","Balloon valvotomy now","No follow-up needed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. All patients with evidence of mitral stenosis on echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult to evaluate them and set the frequency of follow-up. Asymptomatic disease in sinus rhythm needs no therapy yet. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 43, 41"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with chronic mitral regurgitation also has atrial fibrillation and pulmonary hypertension. He is not yet a surgical candidate. Which is part of his medical management?","choices":["Ignoring the rhythm for now","Treating his atrial fibrillation","A nitrate to lower his preload","Balloon valvotomy of the valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Medical treatment of mitral regurgitation aims to increase forward cardiac output while reducing regurgitant volume, and to treat comorbidities such as atrial fibrillation and pulmonary hypertension. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has pulmonic regurgitation on echocardiogram and has developed leg edema and distended neck veins. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Repeat echo in 5 years","Cardiology referral","No follow-up needed","Start lifelong warfarin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is referred when it is seen on echocardiogram or when there are right heart failure signs and symptoms, both of which she has. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman has a holosystolic murmur, and the examiner is unsure whether it is tricuspid or mitral in origin. Which bedside maneuver best separates the two?","choices":["Standing up quickly","Full expiration","Deep inspiration","Sustained handgrip"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inspiration increases venous return to the right heart, so the tricuspid regurgitation murmur grows louder (the Carvallo sign) while mitral regurgitation does not. That distinguishes them. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 71, 9"},{"q":"A 73-year-old woman with moderate aortic stenosis has blood pressure of 158/92 on repeated visits. Her clinician is choosing an antihypertensive. Which class should be avoided?","choices":["An angiotensin receptor blocker","An angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","Low-salt dietary counseling","A beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Aortic stenosis patients are preload dependent; if they have hypertension, beta blockers and calcium channel blockers are avoided, just as nitrates are avoided for angina. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with chronic aortic regurgitation has no symptoms, but his echocardiogram now shows a reduced ejection fraction. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Continue afterload reduction alone","Wait until symptoms develop","Percutaneous balloon valvotomy","Evaluation as a surgical candidate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic aortic regurgitation with a mildly reduced or reduced ejection fraction, with or without symptoms, makes the patient a surgical candidate. Left untreated, survival is only about 2 to 3 years. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman is diagnosed with severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis and asks what she can do at home. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Take daily aspirin","Follow a low-salt diet","Increase fluid and salt","Avoid all walking"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is managed with a low-salt diet together with diuretics and an aldosterone antagonist, which limits the systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man with a harsh systolic murmur has an echocardiogram showing an aortic valve area of 0.8 cm2, a mean gradient of 46 mmHg and a jet velocity of 4.3 m/s. Which severity do these values indicate?","choices":["Ungradeable without catheterization","Severe aortic stenosis","Moderate aortic stenosis","Mild aortic stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Severe aortic stenosis is an area below 1.0 cm2, a mean gradient above 40 mmHg and a jet velocity above 4.0 m/s -- remembered as 4, 40 and 1. All three of his values cross those lines. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 19, 21"},{"q":"A 51-year-old woman with long-standing mitral stenosis develops a hoarse voice and some difficulty swallowing. Which explains her hoarseness?","choices":["Ruptured bronchial vein anastomoses","An enlarged liver from congestion","The enlarged left atrium on the nerve","A dilated aortic root on the trachea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hoarseness in mitral stenosis (Ortner syndrome) comes from the enlarged left atrium impinging on the left recurrent laryngeal nerve; dysphagia comes from the atrium pressing on the esophagus. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman raised in a region where rheumatic fever is common has a quivering discomfort in the neck, cold skin and right upper quadrant pain. There is a soft opening snap (a sharp sound just after the second heart sound) and a mid-diastolic rumble at the left lower sternal border near the xiphoid that grows louder with inspiration. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid regurgitation","Pulmonic regurgitation","Tricuspid stenosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A rumble is stenosis and diastolic narrows it to the mitral or tricuspid valve; the left lower sternal border near the xiphoid and the inspiratory increase make it right-sided, so tricuspid stenosis. Rheumatic fever is its most common cause worldwide. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 67, 65"}]};
